1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
In late 2023, a treatment made with the

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:06,080
gene-editing tool CRISPR was approved for the first

3
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:08,160
time, for sickle cell disease.

4
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:11,840
Since then, the claims have grown: CRISPR will

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:15,520
wipe out malaria, cure every genetic disease, end

6
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:16,480
crop failure.

7
00:00:17,120 --> 00:00:19,360
Some of these could happen, and some are

8
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:19,840
hype.

9
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:23,680
So which future uses of biotechnology are plausible,

10
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:24,800
and how can you tell?

11
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:28,960
CRISPR changes a chosen stretch of DNA using

12
00:00:29,040 --> 00:00:30,040
two parts.

13
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:33,120
A short guide RNA has bases that pair

14
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:36,160
with the target stretch, by normal base pairing.

15
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,560
It leads an enzyme called Cas9 to that

16
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:42,640
spot, and Cas9 cuts both DNA strands.

17
00:00:42,680 --> 00:00:45,040
The cell repairs the cut, but often adds

18
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:47,440
or loses a few bases, which can switch

19
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:48,960
that stretch off.

20
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:51,040
But the guide can sometimes pair with a

21
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:53,920
similar stretch elsewhere, and cut there too.

22
00:00:55,134 --> 00:00:56,853
Now the approved use.

23
00:00:57,134 --> 00:01:00,173
In sickle cell disease, one changed base gives

24
00:01:00,254 --> 00:01:03,053
haemoglobin that makes red cells sickle.

25
00:01:03,214 --> 00:01:05,933
But before birth, you made a different kind,

26
00:01:06,014 --> 00:01:08,893
foetal haemoglobin, which doesn't sickle.

27
00:01:08,974 --> 00:01:11,933
After birth, a protein switches the foetal gene

28
00:01:12,094 --> 00:01:12,973
off.

29
00:01:13,094 --> 00:01:16,413
So scientists take a patient's blood-forming stem cells

30
00:01:16,574 --> 00:01:19,293
and, in the lab, use CRISPR to cut

31
00:01:19,374 --> 00:01:21,693
the DNA that lets these cells make that

32
00:01:21,774 --> 00:01:22,733
switch.

33
00:01:22,814 --> 00:01:24,893
With less of it, the cells make foetal

34
00:01:24,934 --> 00:01:26,413
haemoglobin again.

35
00:01:26,454 --> 00:01:28,653
The sickle allele is still there, but the

36
00:01:28,734 --> 00:01:31,773
foetal haemoglobin keeps most red cells from sickling.

37
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,640
In the main trial, twenty-nine of the thirty

38
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:38,440
patients followed for long enough had no severe

39
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:40,520
pain crises for at least a year.

40
00:01:41,080 --> 00:01:42,600
But look at what it takes.

41
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:46,120
First, strong chemotherapy has to clear out the

42
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,080
patient's own bone marrow, and they spend weeks

43
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:49,800
in hospital.

44
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:52,600
In the United States, it costs over two

45
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:53,880
million dollars.

46
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:56,360
And most babies with the disease are born

47
00:01:56,440 --> 00:01:59,720
in sub-Saharan Africa, where almost no patients can

48
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:00,720
get it yet.

49
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:03,000
Working in a trial isn't the same as

50
00:02:03,080 --> 00:02:04,760
being available to everyone.

51
00:02:05,967 --> 00:02:08,126
So sort each claim by how far the

52
00:02:08,207 --> 00:02:09,166
evidence has got.

53
00:02:09,647 --> 00:02:10,926
Is it only an idea?

54
00:02:11,407 --> 00:02:12,726
Has it worked in the lab?

55
00:02:12,927 --> 00:02:13,806
In animals?

56
00:02:13,967 --> 00:02:15,086
In human trials?

57
00:02:15,487 --> 00:02:17,566
Or is it approved and in use?

58
00:02:18,207 --> 00:02:20,046
The sickle cell treatment is on the top

59
00:02:20,127 --> 00:02:20,526
rung.

60
00:02:21,167 --> 00:02:25,086
Biotechnology is heading in several directions: editing cells

61
00:02:25,287 --> 00:02:28,606
inside the body, editing crops to resist disease,

62
00:02:28,687 --> 00:02:31,926
and screening DNA to spot disease risk early.

63
00:02:32,287 --> 00:02:35,006
But editing works best where one gene causes

64
00:02:35,087 --> 00:02:35,646
the problem.

65
00:02:36,207 --> 00:02:39,726
Common diseases, like type 2 diabetes, involve many

66
00:02:39,807 --> 00:02:42,606
genes and the environment, so there's often no

67
00:02:42,847 --> 00:02:44,446
single stretch to fix.

68
00:02:45,667 --> 00:02:49,426
Malaria, spread by certain mosquitoes, kills hundreds of

69
00:02:49,507 --> 00:02:51,026
thousands of people a year.

70
00:02:51,667 --> 00:02:53,826
Normally, a parent with one copy of an

71
00:02:54,027 --> 00:02:56,786
allele passes it to about half its offspring,

72
00:02:56,907 --> 00:02:59,186
because each gamete gets one chromosome of the

73
00:02:59,267 --> 00:02:59,666
pair.

74
00:03:00,307 --> 00:03:02,226
A gene drive cheats that rule.

75
00:03:02,707 --> 00:03:04,866
The drive allele carries the code for its

76
00:03:04,947 --> 00:03:06,146
own CRISPR editor.

77
00:03:06,707 --> 00:03:08,946
In the cells that make gametes, the editor

78
00:03:09,107 --> 00:03:11,746
cuts the matching spot on the other chromosome,

79
00:03:11,827 --> 00:03:13,906
and the cell repairs the cut by copying

80
00:03:13,987 --> 00:03:15,026
the drive across.

81
00:03:15,507 --> 00:03:17,746
So nearly all the offspring inherit it.

82
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:20,706
Now aim the drive at a gene that

83
00:03:20,727 --> 00:03:23,406
female mosquitoes need to lay eggs.

84
00:03:23,687 --> 00:03:26,086
Females with one copy can still breed and

85
00:03:26,167 --> 00:03:28,806
pass it on, but females with two copies

86
00:03:29,047 --> 00:03:29,846
can't.

87
00:03:30,167 --> 00:03:33,446
As the drive spreads, fewer females can breed,

88
00:03:33,487 --> 00:03:35,686
and the population can crash.

89
00:03:36,087 --> 00:03:38,566
In small lab cages, a drive like this

90
00:03:38,607 --> 00:03:42,166
reached every mosquito, and the populations collapsed within

91
00:03:42,327 --> 00:03:44,566
seven to eleven generations.

92
00:03:44,647 --> 00:03:46,486
But no gene drive has been released into

93
00:03:46,527 --> 00:03:47,446
the wild yet.

94
00:03:48,667 --> 00:03:51,706
So is "gene drives will wipe out malaria"

95
00:03:51,787 --> 00:03:52,666
plausible?

96
00:03:52,987 --> 00:03:53,826
Partly.

97
00:03:54,107 --> 00:03:56,666
The mechanism works in cages, and the need

98
00:03:56,747 --> 00:03:57,626
is huge.

99
00:03:58,187 --> 00:04:00,426
But a mosquito whose cut site has changed

100
00:04:00,587 --> 00:04:03,386
can't be cut, so resistance could stop the

101
00:04:03,467 --> 00:04:04,506
drive spreading.

102
00:04:04,747 --> 00:04:06,666
The cage drive aimed at a stretch that

103
00:04:06,747 --> 00:04:10,666
rarely changes, but wild populations are far bigger.

104
00:04:10,907 --> 00:04:13,866
Removing one mosquito species might affect animals that

105
00:04:13,987 --> 00:04:16,746
eat them, though other mosquitoes would remain.

106
00:04:16,987 --> 00:04:19,466
And a drive can spread across borders, so

107
00:04:19,707 --> 00:04:20,586
who decides?

108
00:04:21,067 --> 00:04:23,066
Once it's out, it may be very hard

109
00:04:23,147 --> 00:04:23,946
to call back.

110
00:04:25,167 --> 00:04:28,766
To judge any future use, ask three questions.

111
00:04:29,087 --> 00:04:32,126
Need: how big is the problem it solves?

112
00:04:32,367 --> 00:04:36,366
Feasibility: can it work safely, at scale, outside

113
00:04:36,407 --> 00:04:37,006
a trial?

114
00:04:37,487 --> 00:04:40,046
Fairness: who will actually get it?

115
00:04:40,447 --> 00:04:44,126
Not "it will", but "it could, if".

116
00:04:44,287 --> 00:04:47,406
Gene drives could reduce malaria in some regions,

117
00:04:47,487 --> 00:04:50,046
if field trials show they're safe and communities

118
00:04:50,127 --> 00:04:50,766
agree.

119
00:04:51,967 --> 00:04:53,406
Two things to hold on to.

120
00:04:53,967 --> 00:04:57,486
Advanced isn't the same as proven: a striking

121
00:04:57,607 --> 00:05:00,046
lab result is a reason to keep testing,

122
00:05:00,127 --> 00:05:01,166
not a promise.

123
00:05:01,727 --> 00:05:05,726
And check need, feasibility and fairness before you

124
00:05:05,807 --> 00:05:07,246
call a benefit likely.

125
00:05:08,467 --> 00:05:09,906
Two exam questions now.

126
00:05:10,467 --> 00:05:10,946
First.

127
00:05:11,507 --> 00:05:14,546
A gene-editing treatment for high cholesterol is being

128
00:05:14,627 --> 00:05:16,946
tested in small, early trials.

129
00:05:17,507 --> 00:05:19,586
It's taken up by liver cells, and it

130
00:05:19,667 --> 00:05:23,826
changes one base in a gene called PCSK9,

131
00:05:23,867 --> 00:05:25,746
so the gene no longer makes a working

132
00:05:25,827 --> 00:05:26,466
protein.

133
00:05:27,107 --> 00:05:30,146
That protein reduces the liver's ability to remove

134
00:05:30,307 --> 00:05:32,146
LDL cholesterol from the blood.

135
00:05:32,867 --> 00:05:35,746
Explain how the edit could lower blood cholesterol.

136
00:05:36,387 --> 00:05:39,226
Then identify two uncertainties.

137
00:05:39,507 --> 00:05:40,786
Pause here to answer.

138
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:54,720
The edit stops liver cells making working PCSK9

139
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:55,440
protein.

140
00:05:55,840 --> 00:05:58,000
Without it, the liver keeps more of its

141
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:00,640
ability to remove LDL, so the level in

142
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:01,840
the blood falls.

143
00:06:02,560 --> 00:06:03,520
Uncertainties:

144
00:06:04,080 --> 00:06:06,640
whether it edits anywhere else in the genome,

145
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:09,200
and whether it stays safe for life, since

146
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:10,400
it can't be undone.

147
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:13,280
Saying the patient's children will inherit it is

148
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:17,120
the classic mistake: liver cells are body cells.

149
00:06:18,334 --> 00:06:18,893
Second.

150
00:06:19,294 --> 00:06:22,653
A headline says: "CRISPR will soon cure all

151
00:06:22,734 --> 00:06:25,693
genetic diseases." Evaluate this claim.

152
00:06:26,174 --> 00:06:27,453
Pause here to answer.

153
00:06:37,867 --> 00:06:41,146
Part of it holds: edited cells already treat

154
00:06:41,227 --> 00:06:43,546
sickle cell, a single-gene disease.

155
00:06:44,107 --> 00:06:46,986
But most common diseases involve many genes and

156
00:06:47,067 --> 00:06:49,706
the environment, most tissues are still hard to

157
00:06:49,787 --> 00:06:52,266
reach, and cost limits who gets it.

158
00:06:52,827 --> 00:06:55,866
So the claim is overstated: CRISPR could treat

159
00:06:56,027 --> 00:06:59,146
some single-gene diseases, if it proves safe and

160
00:06:59,227 --> 00:06:59,866
affordable.

161
00:07:00,507 --> 00:07:03,226
Saying "it's approved, so it works for everyone"

162
00:07:03,307 --> 00:07:04,826
is the classic mistake.

163
00:07:06,034 --> 00:07:10,513
For any future biotechnology, explain the mechanism first:

164
00:07:10,594 --> 00:07:12,513
what it changes, and how.

165
00:07:12,834 --> 00:07:15,233
Then place it on the evidence ladder, judge

166
00:07:15,314 --> 00:07:19,553
need, feasibility and fairness, and write "could, if",

167
00:07:19,634 --> 00:07:21,313
with the condition named.

168
